# SSRS / RDL and SQL Server rules for this repository

These rules apply whenever a change touches an `.rdl` file, generates one,
concerns SSRS / Power BI Paginated report creation, or connects to a SQL Server
to read its schema.

## RDL structure

- Target RDL 2016 (`http://schemas.microsoft.com/sqlserver/reporting/2016/01/reportdefinition`)
  unless the file already declares an older schema; preserve whatever version
  an existing file uses rather than upgrading it silently.
- Element order matters. Inside `DataSet/Query`: `DataSourceName`, then
  `CommandType`, then `CommandText`, in that order, no exceptions.
- `PageHeader` and `PageFooter` are children of `ReportSection/Page`, not of
  `Report`.
- Register the default namespace and the `rd` prefix
  (`http://schemas.microsoft.com/SQLServer/reporting/reportdesigner`) before
  serialising any generated XML.
- Every measurement (`Top`, `Left`, `Width`, `Height`, `PageWidth`,
  `PageHeight`, margins) carries a unit suffix: `in`, `cm`, `mm`, `pt`, or
  `px`. Never emit a bare number.

## Expressions

- Use `=Fields!Name.Value` for a field reference and
  `=Sum(Fields!Name.Value)` (or `Avg`, `Min`, `Max`, `Count`, `CountDistinct`)
  for an aggregate. Do not write an equivalent form that a reader would have
  to parse differently.
- Before referencing `Fields!X.Value` anywhere, confirm `X` is declared as a
  `Field` in the `DataSet` the enclosing region's `DataSetName` points to.
  An unresolved field reference renders `#Error` at runtime with no build-time
  warning, so check it by reading the file, not by assuming.

## Datasets and parameters

- Prefer a shared data source over an embedded connection string when more
  than one report will use it.
- Prefer a stored procedure or a view over inline SQL for any non-trivial
  query.
- Give every `ReportParameter` a `Prompt`, a `DataType`, and a default value
  where one makes sense.
- A multi-value parameter cannot bind directly to a stored procedure
  parameter. Join it into a delimited string
  (`Join(Parameters!P.Value, ",")`) and split it server-side, or use a
  table-valued parameter.

## Anti-patterns to flag in review

`SELECT *`, `SELECT DISTINCT` used to hide a join fan-out, leading-wildcard
`LIKE '%...'`, cross joins, cursors, `IIf` nested more than two levels,
chained `Replace()` calls, embedded `Integrated Security` connection strings,
and more than about five Tablix or Chart regions on one report.

## Before finishing any RDL change

1. Confirm the file still parses as well-formed XML.
2. Confirm every `Fields!X.Value` and every `DataSetName` resolves.
3. Confirm every measurement has a unit.
4. Confirm `PageHeader` / `PageFooter` are inside `Page`.

## SQL Server schema work

These rules apply whenever a change connects to an on-premises SQL Server, or
reads a database's structure to write a query or scope a migration.

- Connect with Windows Integrated Auth: `Trusted_Connection=yes`. Never put a
  username or password in a connection string. `Integrated Security=SSPI` is
  ADO.NET syntax and is silently ignored by ODBC.
- Never hardcode an ODBC driver version. Enumerate installed drivers and take
  the highest `ODBC Driver NN for SQL Server`, falling back to `SQL Server`.
  On driver 18 and above add `Encrypt=yes;TrustServerCertificate=yes`, because
  18 validates the certificate chain by default and most on-premises servers
  are self-signed.
- Read metadata from `sys.*`, never `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`: the ANSI views cannot
  express identity or computed columns, included or filtered index columns, or
  an untrusted foreign key, and truncate routine definitions at 4000 characters.
- Row counts come from `sys.dm_db_partition_stats` filtered to
  `index_id IN (0,1)`, never `COUNT(*)` and never `sys.sysindexes`.
- `max_length` is in bytes, so `nvarchar(50)` reports 100 and `-1` means MAX.
- Foreign keys return one row per column: group by constraint and order by
  `constraint_column_id`, or a composite join clause comes out wrong.
- Resolve an object name to `object_id` via `OBJECT_ID(?)` and filter on the
  integer. Never interpolate a caller-supplied identifier into SQL.
- Keep it read-only: autocommit off, unconditional rollback, and reject any
  statement that is not a single SELECT before sending it.
- Detect soft-delete and type-2 dimension columns before writing a WHERE
  clause. Omitting a soft-delete filter silently returns retired rows; joining
  an SCD2 dimension without a currency filter fans facts out across history.
- Never return values from a column named for an identifier or a health
  attribute (ssn, tax id, passport, licence, card, account number, dob,
  patient, mrn, npi, diagnosis, icd, password, token). Name the column, mark it
  do-not-select, and do not read it.
- Never commit a generated schema digest or skill pack: they name internal
  servers, databases, tables and columns, and this repository is public.

## Full reference

`skills/claude-skills/rdl-generation/` (RDL authoring: structure, datasets,
Tablix/Chart, expressions, units, schema versions, a validator script, and
five working examples),
`skills/claude-skills/ssrs-report-creation/` (the end-to-end workflow:
requirements, data sources, design patterns, export, deployment,
performance, Fabric migration), and
`skills/claude-skills/sql-server-schema/` (connecting to an on-premises SQL
Server, the catalog views, read-only guardrails, schema digests, and
per-database skill packs).
